// The two halves of "a list of lists takes a local row over with push": the
// element-side test that tells the walk a pushed name is a row of a list that
// frees rows shallowly (bit 256, deep/elem/store.id), and the bits the move
// proof refuses (esc_movable, arg.id).

// 1 when `v`, pushed onto the path `b`, is a name of a list and the list at
// the root of `b` is not one that frees its elements by type (a list of
// strings or of structs keeps the stricter rule, esc_strong).
esc_mvel(int b, int v) {
  string pm_ty = type_of(imp_root(b));
  int ok = (import nkind)[v] == "var" && is_list_ty(type_of(v)) == 1 && esc_strong(pm_ty) == 0;
} return int ok;
given esc_mp_fix ((import emp_rv), (import emp_wv)):(1)
given esc_mp_fix ((import emp_rv), (import emp_nv)):(0)
given esc_mp_fix ((import emp_rv), (import emp_pc)):(0)

// The walk flags the move proof refuses in a row: a constructor takes it over
// (bit 64) or a list keeps it too (bit 512), a store that is not a literal
// (2), a keeping use (1), and for a constructor also what a struct frees by
// type (4, 8, 32). A row a list frees shallowly can hold anything, so a push
// is judged on the first three only.
esc_mvmask(int sn) {
  int pm_msk = 559;
  if((import nkind)[sn] == "exprstmt") {
    pm_msk = 67;
  }
} return int pm_msk;
given esc_mp_fix ((import emp_ps)):(67)
given esc_mp_fix ((import emp_wd)):(559)
